1094: ## 24. The lecturer's research run
1095: 
1096: Reframe recorded on 9 October 2026 at the owner's request. Lyceum is not a set of agents that run continuously. It is a workspace in which a lecturer does what a careful lecturer would do anyway, faster and with receipts:
1097: 
1098: 1. **Search.** Choose a source with a stated policy (today: the Greenhouse and Lever employer board APIs, fetched on request through the local API, or a dated saved sample), enter the roles graduates apply to, fetch, and keep the postings worth reading. Each kept posting becomes a source with its link, publication date as published, retrieval time and text fingerprint.
1099: 2. **Read.** Name the skills that matter, in the lecturer's words. The scan proposes the sentence in each posting that uses a term; the lecturer confirms each quotation, and a paraphrase is rejected by the citation validator. The lecturer then vouches that the extraction is complete, and only then does the posting count.
1100: 3. **See the trend.** Observed requirements show the numerator and denominator, the share, a 95% Wilson interval, required against preferred wording, employers, and the course's reviewed coverage stage against its target. Empty evidence is unknown, and no trend over time is shown until a comparable earlier window exists.
1101: 4. **Decide what to do about it.** Map a skill to an outcome, record the stage with cited course evidence, and choose between keeping the course unchanged, a small refresh within existing hours, or a formal revision.
1102: 5. **Take it to the meeting.** Prepare a meeting brief with the decision requested, the evidence base, the table, the options, the lecturer's notes, the limitations and every receipt. Download it as Markdown, or send it to a colleague inside Lyceum, where it arrives on their Home page with the receipts attached.
1103: 
1104: Implemented on 9 October: all five steps, in Course Studio under Evidence and on the Home page for recipients. Proofs cover the collector (host allow-list, token validation, one-hour cache, rate limits, size and timeout limits, payload normalisation), the scan (exact substrings, requirement heuristic, no double counting), bulk review, briefs, notifications and backups. The self-driving demo plays this storyline on the saved sample. Live fetches were verified on 9 October against the Stripe board on Greenhouse (727 postings, 8 "data analyst" titles) and the Palantir site on Lever (313 postings).
1105: 
1106: Still pending from Section 7: Scrapling page adapters for approved employer pages, snapshots of arbitrary sites, cross-site vacancy clustering, scheduled refresh with refresh-failure reporting, and contextual sources beyond manual paste. The seven branded agents remain product labels for roles in this workflow; none of them runs on its own.
1107: 
1108: What changed in the claims: Lyceum no longer says that agents monitor the market. It says that the lecturer runs a research run when a decision needs one, and that every number can be traced to a quotation the lecturer confirmed.
1109: 
1110: ## 25. Lyceum inside the tools universities already use
1111: 
1112: The owner's commercial question: a university already has an LMS, Teams or Slack, a curriculum system and a dozen other tools, so why add one more? The answer the plan now takes: do not ask them to. Lyceum should be reachable from the tools they already open, with the web workspace kept for the work that needs a full editor.
1113: 
1114: | Surface | Good for | Poor at | Needs |
1115: |---|---|---|---|
1116: | Web workspace (today) | Editing outlines, confirming quotations, reviewing tables, preparing briefs | Nothing it does better elsewhere | The local API for live boards |
1117: | Microsoft Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot through a Copilot Studio agent | Asking what employers want in a role, receiving a brief in a channel, escalating to a coordinator, approvals in chat | Editing an outline; confirming dozens of quotations; anything that needs a table with controls | Copilot Studio reaches external systems through a custom connector or a REST API tool defined from an OpenAPI description, with Entra ID sign-in; publishing to Teams goes through the Channels page and admin approval |
1118: | Slack app | The same conversational asks and escalations in Slack-first universities | The same editing limits | A small Slack app with slash commands and link unfurls over the same API |
1119: | LMS through LTI 1.3 | Launching the course workspace from the course page; sharing a brief as course material | Institution-wide analytics | An LMS test site and administrator configuration |
1120: 
1121: The shared piece is one small API over a shared store: collect a board, list evidence, produce a report or brief, send a brief, record a decision. That API is the W02 server work, which now has a concrete reason to exist. Sequencing stays as decided: local-first for the competition demo, the shared API and identity when a pilot has a date, then the Teams or Slack surface for the pilot institution's actual tool.
1122: 
1123: Efficiency and performance differ by surface, and the pitch should say so: the chat surfaces are for asking and escalating, the workspace is for editing and confirming, and the file exports keep working everywhere. Copilot Studio also carries licensing and admin-approval steps that the institution's IT team controls; these are costs of adoption, not features. Nothing in this section is implemented yet. It is the integration strategy and the commercial answer to "why another tool". Sources are in Section 29.
1124: 
1125: ## 26. Pitch structure: the AWS five V's
1126: 
1127: The owner asked for the five V framing seen in an AWS presentation. The closest published framework is the AWS Machine Learning Blog's five V's for taking AI projects from pilot to production (24 October 2025): Value, Visualize, Validate, Verify, Venture. It is a delivery framework rather than an investor template, which suits a technology-readiness jury. The mapping:
1128: 
1129: | V | The framework's question | Lyceum's answer, with evidence |
1130: |---|---|---|
1131: | Value | Is this worth solving? | Lecturers must refresh courses against employer demand and defend the change to colleagues and reviewers; today the evidence lives in browser tabs and memory. Malaysian graduates face a persistent qualification-to-job mismatch (DOSM reports 32.2% underemployment among graduates in 2024), and MOHE reviews courses for relevance. The pain is felt by lecturers, coordinators and quality units. |
1132: | Visualize | How will we know it worked? | The pilot measures in Section 17: task completion, time to an accepted brief, zero fabricated citations in a checked sample, review effort, repeat use. Baselines come from the lecturer's current process. |
1133: | Validate | How do we build it? | Built and checked: on-request collection from two documented board APIs, the exact-quotation validator, honest denominators, briefs and escalation; 149 automated checks, four browser smokes and a self-driving demo on a dated sample of real postings. No model key is required for any of it. |
1134: | Verify | How do we run it at scale? | Local-first today; a shared API and identity when a pilot has a date; Teams or Slack surfaces through that API; source policies, retention and audit records designed in from the start. |
1135: | Venture | How do we fund it through to value? | Near-zero variable cost for the deterministic path; optional AI and media behind caps; a department pilot before any institution licence; the market sizing in Section 27. The ask is a pilot with 3 to 5 lecturers. |
1136: 
1137: Use the five V's as the deck's spine when the audience is a readiness or innovation jury. Keep the Section 19 script when the slot is five minutes and the audience is approving a direction.
1138: 
1139: ## 27. Market sizing: labelled estimates
1140: 
1141: Everything here is an estimate from public sources checked on 9 October 2026. The sources disagree on definitions, and none of them measures willingness to pay for Lyceum. Treat the figures as the shape of the market, not a forecast.
1142: 
1143: **Units.** The user is a lecturer; the buyer is a department, faculty or central teaching unit; the licence unit is most likely an institution or a department, not a seat.
1144: 
1145: | Layer | Definition | Figure and source | Caveat |
1146: |---|---|---|---|
1147: | TAM, world | Accredited higher education institutions and their teaching staff | Over 22,000 accredited institutions and 269 million students in 2024 (UNESCO global roadmap); over 14 million tertiary teachers (UNESCO IESALC) | Institution counts range from 18,000 to 88,000 depending on definition |
1148: | Adjacent spend | Curriculum and catalogue management software for higher education | 2025 estimates from USD 0.48 billion to USD 3.5 billion with 7 to 9 percent annual growth (Report Prime, Dataintelo, MarketIntelo, 360iResearch) | A sevenfold spread between publishers; none isolates labour-market alignment |
1149: | SAM, Malaysia | Lecturers and institutions reachable with a file-first tool that fits MQA course reviews | MOHE 2023 statistics: 22,370 lecturers in public universities, 19,743 in private institutions, 2,772 in polytechnics, roughly 45,000 before research fellows and assistant lecturers. Institutions: 20 public universities; 65 private universities, 38 university colleges and 252 colleges (MIDA, February 2026); polytechnics and community colleges in addition | The 2024 MOHE tables exist but the staff figures were not legible in the PDF extraction; verify against Chapter 1, Table 1.6 |
1150: | SOM, first two years | Departments that run a pilot and convert | 3 to 5 pilot institutions, 50 to 200 lecturers, one or two paid department licences | A target, not a forecast |
1151: 
1152: **Illustrative arithmetic, not a projection.** If a department licence were priced at RM 6,000 to RM 15,000 a year and a pilot converted five departments, year-two revenue would be RM 30,000 to RM 75,000. The Malaysian ceiling at that price, across roughly 400 institutions with several departments each, is in the low millions of ringgit a year. This is why Section 25 matters: the cost of adoption must be close to zero for the market to be worth serving, and the competitive answer is to fit into existing tools rather than to add a platform.
1153: 
1154: **Competitors and positioning.** Lightcast Skillabi (alignment scores from course text against a large labour-market dataset, pricing on request), Chmura JobsEQ (programme alignment through CIP to SOC crosswalks) and the curriculum suites (Ellucian, Watermark, CourseLeaf, Modern Campus, Coursedog) sit above the lecturer: institutional licences, global datasets, scores whose formula is not published. Lyceum sits at the lecturer's desk: receipts the lecturer confirmed, denominators in view, a brief for the meeting, and a path into Teams or Slack. It can later ingest a licensed dataset as another source with a policy; it does not need to replace one. Claims of novelty stay at defensible differentiation, as Section 6 already says.
1155: 
1156: **Why now, in Malaysia.** DOSM counted 5.98 million graduates in 2024 with unemployment at 3.2 percent but underemployment at 32.2 percent; TalentCorp's MyCOL 2024/2025 lists 66 critical occupations; MOHE reviews courses for relevance. These are reasons a department would fund a pilot, not proof that it will. Sources are listed in Section 29.
1157: 
1158: ## 28. Further improvements identified on 9 October
1159: 
1160: In priority order after the research run:
1161: 
1162: 1. Quarantine the synthetic screens. Trends, the public Relevance Index, the Acceptance test and the Closed loop still run on fixtures from the earlier storyline. Keep them reachable for the proofs, but move them out of the lecturer's navigation behind a research sandbox label so that the first thing a judge opens is the real workflow.
1163: 2. Provider drift in W06: upgrade the Anthropic SDK, replace the forced tool call in the intake route with structured outputs, set a current model id, and surface payload truncation.
1164: 3. Comparable time windows: store each collection run as a dated window so that a later run can report a change in percentage points with both denominators, as Section 8.1 specifies.
1165: 4. Employer naming for Lever boards: the API does not state the employer, so the site token is used; allow renaming on import.
1166: 5. Brief export to DOCX and PDF in addition to Markdown, for meetings that will not open a Markdown file.
1167: 6. Code-split the bundle; the main chunk now exceeds 500 kB.
1168: 7. An accessibility pass with NVDA on the research run, which has more controls than any other section.
1169: 8. An OpenAPI description of the collect, report and brief operations, as the first artefact of the extension strategy and the contract for W02.
1170: 9. A deck in the five-V structure, a recording of the new Play demo, and a rehearsal on the presentation machine.
1171: 
1: # Lyceum v3: as-built report, 9 October 2026
2: 
3: This report describes the prototype as it exists and was verified on 9 October 2026. It replaces the July 2026 as-built document, which is kept in `docs/history/` for the record and describes an earlier storyline that the current build no longer claims. Every statement here was checked against the running code on the owner's machine on 9 October; the verification table lists the commands and their results.
4: 
5: ## 1. Summary
6: 
7: Lyceum is a lecturer's course workspace. It helps a lecturer gather what employers ask for from permitted sources, keep every requirement as an exact quotation from its source, count honestly with the denominator in view, map skills to course outcomes with cited course evidence, revise outcomes and topics under explicit constraints, prepare teaching sessions, and take a meeting brief with every receipt to colleagues. Nothing runs by itself, nothing changes a course without the lecturer, and no AI key is needed for any of it.
8: 
9: The product runs locally: a React and TypeScript web workspace with a browser-local store and validated JSON backups, plus a thin Node and Hono API that parses documents and fetches postings from two documented public job-board APIs on request. It is a working local prototype with automated verification. It is not yet a shared, multi-user service.
10: 
11: ## 2. Problem and positioning
12: 
13: A lecturer who wants to refresh a course has three jobs: find out what employers actually ask for, work out what the course already introduces, practises and assesses, and defend the change to colleagues and the quality committee. Today that evidence lives in browser tabs, inboxes and memory, and the decision is hard to explain later. In Malaysia, DOSM counted 5.98 million graduates in 2024 with unemployment at 3.2 percent but underemployment at 32.2 percent, and MOHE reviews courses for relevance.
14: 
15: Lyceum is advisory. Lecturers accept, edit, reject or defer every suggestion. Formal syllabus approval stays in the university's existing process. Lyceum does not claim to monitor the labour market, score employability, predict learning gains or certify accreditation.
16: 
17: ## 3. What a lecturer can do today
18: 
19: **Home.** Resume the latest teaching plan, see the work queue for the current role, read notifications. An evidence brief sent by a colleague opens here with its receipts and can be downloaded.
20: 
21: **Courses.** Search and filter the catalogue, open an assigned course, or create a personal course workspace with a code, title and optional programme. A personal workspace is private preparation, not an official course.
22: 
23: **Course Studio, Outcomes and topics.** Start a revision from the current version; add, edit or remove course learning outcomes with Bloom level, achievement evidence and programme outcome mappings; add topics with hours, weeks, outcome links, prerequisites and protected foundations. Save incomplete drafts. Check constraints: broken links, prerequisite cycles and reversed order, exceeded contact or self-study budgets and removed protected topics block a candidate; unknown budgets stay visible warnings. Compare before and after, record a rationale and teaching term, then explicitly adopt the candidate as the local course version. The baseline, the version history and the decision record are preserved; affected teaching sessions and assessments are flagged for review rather than rewritten.
24: 
25: **Course Studio, Evidence.** The research run:
26: 1. Search. Choose a source with a stated policy: the Greenhouse job board API, the Lever postings API, or a dated saved sample of real postings. Enter role keywords, fetch, keep the postings worth reading and import them. Each imported posting is a source with its title, employer, link, publication date as published, retrieval time, text and text fingerprint. Identical texts become duplicate copies and count once.
27: 2. Read. Name the skills that matter, with aliases. The scan proposes, per posting and term, the first sentence that uses the term, as an exact substring, with a heuristic reading of required or preferred that the lecturer can change. The lecturer confirms or dismisses each quotation. A quotation that is not in the retained text is rejected, including hand-typed paraphrases. Every stored quotation is re-verified on display and on backup import.
28: 3. Review. The lecturer vouches that a posting's extraction is complete. Only reviewed postings enter the denominator.
29: 4. See the trend. Observed requirements show, per skill, the numerator and denominator, the share, a 95 percent Wilson interval, required against preferred wording, distinct employers, contextual documents, the reviewed coverage stage against its target, and the shortfall. Empty evidence is unknown, never zero. A demand-weighted coverage index is reported with the share of quoted weight whose stage or target is unknown. No trend over time is shown until a comparable earlier window exists.
30: 5. Decide and brief. Map a skill to one or more course outcomes with a documented stage and cited course evidence. Prepare a meeting brief with the decision requested, the evidence base, the table, the options, the lecturer's notes, the limitations and every receipt. Download it as Markdown or as editable slides (one receipt slide per source), or send it to colleagues inside Lyceum. The evidence report itself also exports as Markdown with receipts.
31: 
32: A document the lecturer is permitted to keep can also be pasted in as a source.
33: 
34: **Course Studio, Teaching plan.** Save sessions with week, topic, duration, outcome links, activity, learning check and resources; ready and delivered sessions require preparation details. Export the plan as Markdown. Any saved session downloads as an editable teaching pack (.pptx): a title slide, the session's outcomes, the employer evidence linked to those outcomes with exact quotations, the activity, the check and the resources, each with speaker notes. No model writes a word of it. A course change flags affected sessions for review while keeping their content.
35: 
36: **Course Studio, Assessment and Proposal.** Prepare a blueprint without a proposal; item generation uses local templates. An affected assessment needs a recorded course-change review and repaired links before another submission. The Proposal tab retains the earlier review workflow, whose scenario results are illustrative.
37: 
38: **Guide and progress.** Section guides for every screen, a role filter, search, the list of unbuilt work, and workspace backup and restore: a schema-versioned JSON file with a preview, validation of every record and link, a replacement acknowledgement and a pre-restore recovery copy. Older backup versions restore with later collections empty.
39: 
40: **The self-driving demo.** Play demo on the sign-in screen plays a full tour of CS310 over the real app with a simulated cursor, in about five and a half minutes after three intro cards: a course revision under constraint checks with its version history, the research run on the saved sample, exact quotations with a rejected paraphrase, bulk review, a coverage mapping, a teaching session with its slide pack downloaded, a template assessment, a meeting brief with slides sent to the programme owner, the guide and backup page, and the recipient reading the brief.
41: 
