+# Lyceum: a course decision with receipts
+
+## Spoken talk track
+
+Seven minutes, including clicks and pauses. Dr Aisyah is a fictional computer science lecturer in Malaysia, not a pilot participant. Stage timings are rehearsal allocations, not measured product performance. Source keys refer to the register below.
+
+Use the existing prototype persona and example course, explaining their status. Rehearse with a saved revision and teaching session, explicitly labelled prepared examples. Keep the real-posting sample ready. Say whether collection is live, cached or sampled. Show actual controls and artifacts, never an invented agent conversation. The roles follow `runs.ts`; the older review assigns quotation reading differently.
+
+| Slide | Time | Spoken words | Judge sees and what is real |
+|---|---:|---|---|
+| Afternoon | 35 seconds | “Imagine Dr Aisyah after her afternoon class. She has a course outline open, employer postings in browser tabs, and a message asking what she wants to change before the next review. She can suggest another dashboard exercise. But when a colleague asks, ‘Why this change?’, she needs evidence she can bring into the room. Lyceum gives that afternoon a destination: a course decision with receipts.” | Illustrated desk, tabs converging on a meeting brief. Fictional story, no testimonial. |
+| Beyond her desk | 45 seconds | “The context reaches beyond her desk. DOSM reports Malaysian graduate underemployment at 32.2% in 2024. TalentCorp lists 66 critical occupations in MyCOL 2024/2025. Across Asia and the Pacific, the ILO reports youth unemployment at 13.9% in 2023. Worldwide, employers surveyed by the World Economic Forum expect 39% of workers’ skills to change or become outdated by 2030. These describe different populations, not a league table. They do not prove curricula caused the problem. They explain why course decisions deserve better evidence.” | Map zooms from Malaysia to Asia-Pacific to world. Labels and figures appear sequentially, with sources in speaker notes. Sources: Pain, Review. |
+| Folio | 25 seconds | “Let’s follow her work. After login, we choose a course, inspect its outcomes, then head toward evidence and an output. Folio is the course reader. Here are the saved outcomes and topics. The lecturer checks them. This is an example course in a local prototype, not a verified institutional record.” | Home, Courses, Outcomes and topics. Presenter traces the route with the pointer. Saved course data is real; persona and course are examples. No claim that proposed onboarding is shipped. |
+| Scout | 30 seconds | “Scout is the industry researcher. I ask it to fetch a permitted employer board, then keep relevant postings. Watch the publication date, retrieval time and source link. If we use the saved sample, I will say so. Collection happens when I ask. This is evidence from a selected board, not continuous monitoring of the labour market.” | Evidence: choose Greenhouse and Stripe, search data analyst, fetch and import relevant results. Display source policy and provenance. Label cache or sample honestly. |
+| Atlas | 35 seconds | “Atlas helps read and map. I name a skill, inspect the proposed sentence, then confirm its exact wording. Would you accept this paraphrase as a quotation? Watch it fail. Now I keep the exact passage and link the skill to a course outcome, with the course evidence beside it. I supply and confirm that mapping. A wording match cannot judge my interpretation for me.” | Scan, retained passage, rejected paraphrase, valid quotation, lecturer-entered mapping. Ask judges by show of hands; proceed without waiting for discussion. No autonomous confirmation. |
+| Lens | 25 seconds | “Once I have checked extraction and marked postings reviewed, Lens counts. Here is the skill numerator over unique reviewed postings, with a 95% Wilson interval. Unknown coverage stays unknown. The interval does not fix selection bias. This is documented course coverage, not measured student attainment.” | Mark reviewed, point to actual numerator, denominator, exclusions, interval and coverage target. Do not script or invent a sample count. Lens is the evidence analyst, not a validated student predictor. |
+| Quill | 30 seconds | “Quill is the course designer. Here is a prepared revision, with the before and after visible. I run the constraint check: hours, links, prerequisites and protected foundations. Unknown budgets remain warnings. I review the rationale and choose whether to adopt locally. Passing these checks is not accreditation. Formal approval still belongs to the university.” | Saved draft, differences, constraint results, local adoption and version history. Lecturer-authored prepared example; real deterministic checks. |
+| Prism | 25 seconds | “Prism turns the saved teaching session into something Aisyah can use. Outcomes, activity, learning check, and linked employer evidence travel into an editable PowerPoint pack with speaker notes. I download it and open it. No model wrote this pack. Its usefulness still depends on the lecturer’s preparation.” | Teaching plan, prepared saved session, teaching-pack download, editable text in exported PPTX. Real deterministic export. |
+| Steward | 25 seconds | “Steward prepares the meeting brief: the proposed decision, limitations and receipts. I download the brief as slides and show its sources. Sending inside this prototype creates a local recipient notification. It is not shared institutional messaging. Aisyah now has something her colleague can inspect, question and revise.” | Prepare a fresh brief and export immediately from unchanged evidence. Show local recipient notification. Avoid historical-brief exports with potentially different current evidence. |
+| Why this project | 30 seconds | “The novelty is this connected, lecturer-controlled chain. Exact quotations are the unit of evidence. Counts keep their denominators and intervals. The core works without AI keys, and the outputs stay editable. The research sandbox stays labelled as synthetic and unvalidated. Alignment software already exists. Our contribution is making the path from source to teaching artifact inspectable at the lecturer’s desk.” | Quotation, count, mapping, revision and pack as a connected visual. Label sandbox clearly; do not show synthetic scores as results. Real local build, not shared service. |
+| Market and route | 45 seconds | “Our plan’s estimated TAM is over 22,000 institutions and over 14 million tertiary teachers worldwide. The Malaysian SAM is roughly 45,000 lecturers. Our SOM target for the first two years is 3 to 5 pilot institutions, 50 to 200 lecturers and one or two paid department licences. These are targets, not sales forecasts. Skillabi offers alignment scores, JobsEQ programme alignment, and curriculum suites institutional workflows. Those do not by themselves establish this lecturer-confirmed receipt chain. We can complement them. Next comes shared identity and an API, then Teams, Slack or the LMS. Those integrations are planned.” | Nested market shapes, then workspace connected to future surfaces. Source details stay in notes and handout. No invented revenue chart. Sources: Plan, Pack. |
+| Benchmark before belief | 45 seconds | “The student-simulation benchmark has not run. We propose one course, three outcomes and two successive cohorts of approximately 30 consenting learners each. First define attainment for each outcome and assessment. Use the earlier cohort for development, freeze the model, then test on the later cohort against prior-attainment and prior-performance baselines. Compare probability error and calibration. Surveys measure confidence and difficulty separately from assessed attainment. Report response rates, missing data and uncertainty. This is a feasibility study. It would not establish that a course change causes learning gains.” | Empty benchmark diagram: assessment evidence and surveys in separate lanes, frozen model, held-out evaluation. No fabricated results. Source: Review. |
+| The ask | 25 seconds | “I am asking for the travel fellowship to present Lyceum and recruit pilot lecturers. We will measure task completion, citation integrity, time to a usable brief and repeat use against their current process. We will also test who buys and what adoption requires. Help us return with measured evidence. Aisyah keeps the decision. Lyceum brings the receipts.” | Brief returns to the illustrated desk. Contact and event details supplied by presenter before delivery. No invented partner or funding amount. |
+
+## Per-slide visual notes
+
+Each row gives the complete authored slide text, below twelve words. No extra title, footer or decorative statistic. Sources belong in notes and handout. Live application content is inspected evidence, not slide copy; use no text overlay on the live screen. Do not conceal provenance to shorten a live view.
+
+| Slide | On-slide text | Graphic or live replacement |
+|---|---|---|
+| Afternoon | “Dr Aisyah’s afternoon. Fictional lecturer. Real workflow.” | Draw the desk, scattered evidence and unfinished brief. |
+| Beyond her desk | “Malaysia 32.2%. Asia-Pacific youth 13.9%. World skills 39%.” | Sequential map panels, no comparative bars across unlike measures. Years and definitions are spoken and in notes. |
+| Folio | “Folio: inspect the course” | Switch to app; pointer guides Home, course, outcomes. |
+| Scout | “Scout: collect on request” | Source card and posting provenance, with live status visible. |
+| Atlas | “Atlas: confirm wording, document the mapping” | Passage and quotation, then skill-to-outcome connection. |
+| Lens | “Lens: counts with denominators” | Actual observed-requirements row, interval and coverage. |
+| Quill | “Quill: revise, check, decide” | Before/after outline and constraint result. |
+| Prism | “Prism: editable teaching materials” | Saved session becomes an opened editable slide. |
+| Steward | “Steward: take receipts to the meeting” | Fresh brief, receipt appendix, local notification. |
+| Why this project | “Exact quotations. Human decisions. Editable outputs. Research sandbox: synthetic, unvalidated.” | Artifact chain with sandbox visibly separated. |
+| Market and route | “TAM. SAM. SOM. Teams, Slack, LMS: planned.” | Nested shapes, then future connections, no proportional areas implied. |
+| Benchmark before belief | “Benchmark not yet run. Assessments and surveys stay separate.” | Empty evaluation diagram, no result bars. |
+| The ask | “Travel fellowship. Recruit pilots. Return with measured evidence.” | Completed brief beside Aisyah’s course outline. |
+
+## Delivery notes
+
+Open warmly, as though describing a colleague’s afternoon. Pause after “Why this change?” Look up before revealing Lyceum. Build enthusiasm through visible actions: invite the quotation challenge, let the rejection register, then show the useful export.
+
+Say slower: “The interval does not fix selection bias.” Also slow down for “The student-simulation benchmark has not run.” Look at the judges for both statements and for the ask. Stop talking while an export opens. Avoid narrating every click.
+
+If asked “Is this just AI slop?”, answer: “Judge the artifact with me. Open its source, inspect the exact quotation, check the denominator and edit the output. The deterministic core needs no AI key. A matching quotation can still be misinterpreted, so the lecturer retains responsibility. The simulation remains unvalidated.” Demonstrate the source rather than arguing about AI.
+
+Preflight downloads and prepared examples. If collection fails, disclose the dated sample; if the app fails, narrate the existing backup recording and identify it as recorded. Do not create the impression of a live run. Keep synthetic screens labelled Research sandbox in presentation material; this document does not implement navigation changes.
+
+## One-page judge handout outline
+
+- **Top:** Aisyah’s fictional afternoon, the fragmented evidence problem, and a small source-to-brief diagram.
+- **Centre:** Seven role cards from Folio through Steward, showing input, output and lecturer decision. Place Lens after Atlas as in the demonstration. Include a real quotation with source locator, a denominator and an editable artifact reference, copied from the demonstrated run rather than invented here.
+- **Evidence boundary:** Working local prototype, deterministic core, browser-local records. Prepared examples identified. Research sandbox synthetic. Student benchmark not run. Shared accounts and integrations planned.
+- **Market strip:** TAM, SAM and SOM from the register, labelled estimates or targets. Lecturer is user; department, faculty or teaching unit is buyer. Skillabi, JobsEQ and curriculum suites provide adjacent capabilities; differentiation is the confirmed evidence chain, not an invented category.
+- **Research strip:** Interview lecturers and coordinators across Asian institutions; observe time, handoffs and missing evidence. Test willingness to pay and procurement. Course intelligence comes from identified boards and reviewed samples, not whole-market surveillance.
+- **Bottom:** Benchmark design, pilot measures, travel fellowship ask, presenter contact and source links. No outcome promise.
